:{"position":[-10.367434150559887,2.6814006179128858,-129.51912979006698],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":48,"seed":243}},{"id":"3554f478-7ea3-4d5b-9333-a7b10a25b52a","createdAt":1786986001848,"transform":{"position":[37.82658645265142,-2.41323197892918,-140.2830706080776],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":36,"seed":256}},{"id":"879cf629-6ac2-406d-9578-72a8391a184d","createdAt":1786986001850,"transform":{"position":[66.41611777841231,-0.44677431241920784,-98.03061476237741],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":40,"seed":269}},{"id":"07cd1534-7cf9-4155-b5e9-cb5507a902b3","createdAt":1786986001851,"transform":{"position":[106.1387102260257,0.28431287409174866,-73.98726812013379],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":44,"seed":282}},{"id":"9da4b010-b320-4540-ba7a-7d27d6eccd15","createdAt":1786986001852,"transform":{"position":[95.58782553434312,0.43639826398776793,-26.886372129051338],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"araucaria-tree","options":{"height":48,"seed":295}},{"id":"681eae1d-3c7a-46d0-a04a-3652a93f4ced","createdAt":1786986001853,"transform":{"position":[-11,-8.215650382226158e-15,37],"rotation":[0,0.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-11,"z":37,"rotY":0.3,"eyebrow":"Where you are","title":"The last day of the dinosaurs","body":"Everything here lived about 66 million years ago, right at the end of the Cretaceous. Every animal on this island really did share that time and place — most famous dinosaurs never met each other."}},{"id":"75787d17-9c02-4262-84c4-13f784aaf49a","createdAt":1786986001855,"transform":{"position":[11,-8.215650382226158e-15,37],"rotation":[0,-0.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":11,"z":37,"rotY":-0.3,"eyebrow":"Deep time","title":"Stegosaurus was already ancient","body":"More time separates Stegosaurus from T. rex than separates T. rex from you. The Age of Dinosaurs lasted so long that its own history had ancient history."}},{"id":"42125513-f1b0-4b29-ab15-2cd016edd062","createdAt":1786986001856,"transform":{"position":[-10,-3.774758283725532e-15,17],"rotation":[0,0.4,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-10,"z":17,"rotY":0.4,"eyebrow":"Underfoot","title":"No grass anywhere","body":"Grass had barely appeared. The green here is ferns, horsetails and moss — which is why almost every plant-eater on this island is built to browse low shrubs, not to graze a lawn."}},{"id":"15b4e2d5-b6d1-4a2f-be8a-722f27af5c94","createdAt":1786986001857,"transform":{"position":[10,-3.774758283725532e-15,17],"rotation":[0,-0.4,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":10,"z":17,"rotY":-0.4,"eyebrow":"Something new","title":"The first flowers","body":"Flowering plants were a recent invention in the Cretaceous. The magnolias here are among the oldest kinds still alive — T. rex lived alongside the very first blossom."}},{"id":"01851211-f5d2-4290-9142-018c6b5f0cb3","createdAt":1786986001859,"transform":{"position":[15,-4.218847493575595e-15,19],"rotation":[0,-0.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":15,"z":19,"rotY":-0.5,"eyebrow":"The dig","title":"How anyone knows any of this","body":"The string grid is not decoration. Every bone is drawn and measured inside its own square before it is lifted, because where a bone sat tells you as much as the bone does."}},{"id":"cb43e426-17fc-4aca-9a23-363727acba66","createdAt":1786986001860,"transform":{"position":[39,-0.0004227370890805826,27],"rotation":[0,-2.6,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":39,"z":27,"rotY":-2.6,"eyebrow":"The dig","title":"How a fossil forms","body":"An animal has to be buried fast — under river mud or sand — before it rots or is eaten. Minerals then seep in over millions of years. Almost nothing that dies ever becomes a fossil."}},{"id":"9f9a76e2-60bb-4b17-84a4-a8f99f17de13","createdAt":1786986001861,"transform":{"position":[24,3.552713678800501e-15,-16],"rotation":[0,-1.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":24,"z":-16,"rotY":-1.5,"eyebrow":"The dig","title":"Plaster jackets","body":"Those white bundles are bones wrapped in plaster and burlap, exactly like a cast on a broken arm. It is the only way to move something that has been shattered for 66 million years."}},{"id":"2717fd3d-327a-4232-9050-2fae542a719d","createdAt":1786986001863,"transform":{"position":[-21,-4.440892098500626e-16,2],"rotation":[0,1.4,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-21,"z":2,"rotY":1.4,"eyebrow":"Triceratops","title":"Three horns and a shield","body":"The frill is bone, and the brow horns are over three feet long. They were probably used against rivals as much as against predators — plenty of frills carry healed wounds from other Triceratops."}},{"id":"04c6d648-927e-4a00-8d51-79365540be44","createdAt":1786986001864,"transform":{"position":[-18,3.1086244689504383e-15,-14],"rotation":[0,2.2,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-18,"z":-14,"rotY":2.2,"eyebrow":"Ankylosaurus","title":"A living tank","body":"Armour plates set into the skin, spikes along both flanks, and a solid bone club on the tail heavy enough to break a leg. Even its eyelids were armoured."}},{"id":"a11a3736-3224-4bf2-b312-b08af3bd2b33","createdAt":1786986001865,"transform":{"position":[-46,-0.02314646147616939,-18],"rotation":[0,1.1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-46,"z":-18,"rotY":1.1,"eyebrow":"Edmontosaurus","title":"What T. rex ate","body":"Some Edmontosaurus fossils carry T. rex bite marks that HEALED. That is the strongest evidence we have that T. rex attacked living prey — and that sometimes the prey got away."}},{"id":"8ba49807-f07d-4eeb-8555-9e0eaa933b94","createdAt":1786986001866,"transform":{"position":[9,3.1086244689504383e-15,-14],"rotation":[0,-1.2,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":9,"z":-14,"rotY":-1.2,"eyebrow":"Pachycephalosaurus","title":"Ten inches of solid skull","body":"Stand next to this one — it is about your height. The dome on its head is bone up to ten inches thick, probably for shoving contests with rivals rather than head-on charges."}},{"id":"b653c627-41c1-4c22-a115-fb200724674f","createdAt":1786986001868,"transform":{"position":[25,6.8833827526759706e-15,-31],"rotation":[0,-1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":25,"z":-31,"rotY":-1,"eyebrow":"Footprints","title":"What tracks tell you","body":"A skeleton tells you how an animal was built. A trackway tells you what it did — how fast it walked, whether it travelled alone, and how it placed its feet."}},{"id":"c0fbf10b-8b9c-4172-a3b0-46ff0dfe3492","createdAt":1786986001869,"transform":{"position":[-21,-0.010479682493685916,-43],"rotation":[0,0.5,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-21,"z":-43,"rotY":0.5,"eyebrow":"The nest","title":"Dinosaur parents","body":"Dinosaurs built nests, sat on their eggs and looked after what hatched. That behaviour is one of the strongest links between them and the birds outside your window."}},{"id":"c19243aa-7589-4f4b-862b-1350b29500b8","createdAt":1786986001870,"transform":{"position":[25,-0.6158072115222735,-65],"rotation":[0,-2.3,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":25,"z":-65,"rotY":-2.3,"eyebrow":"Tyrannosaurus rex","title":"Thirteen feet at the hip","body":"Nearly three times your height at the hip, and forty feet nose to tail. The biggest teeth found are about the size of a banana — and built like railway spikes, for crushing bone rather than slicing."}},{"id":"fd690099-dd39-4990-b9e2-e0ef60d217f7","createdAt":1786986001871,"transform":{"position":[-13,-0.560242708696586,-58],"rotation":[0,1.1,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-13,"z":-58,"rotY":1.1,"eyebrow":"Tyrannosaurus rex","title":"Those arms are not a joke","body":"They are shorter than yours, but each one could curl around 400 pounds. Nobody is certain what they were for — holding struggling prey and pushing up off the ground are the best guesses."}},{"id":"d3a8c6c3-02aa-44dd-8f97-ccd8e47b1848","createdAt":1786986001873,"transform":{"position":[33,-0.1482357364988014,-47],"rotation":[0,-1.9,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":33,"z":-47,"rotY":-1.9,"eyebrow":"Look up","title":"That is not a dinosaur","body":"Quetzalcoatlus is a pterosaur — a flying cousin, not a dinosaur. Its wings are skin stretched on one enormous finger. Standing on the ground it was as tall as a giraffe."}},{"id":"acf7f84b-f8cd-419c-871d-e5f9d4f2b3fd","createdAt":1786986001874,"transform":{"position":[-36,-4.440892098500626e-15,20],"rotation":[0,0.9,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-36,"z":20,"rotY":0.9,"eyebrow":"Living fossil","title":"You can go and touch one","body":"The ginkgo here is near-identical to the ginkgo growing in car parks today. Whatever wiped out the dinosaurs, this tree walked straight through it unchanged."}},{"id":"adb7b02c-2393-4e62-8c4a-e477cd8b934f","createdAt":1786986001875,"transform":{"position":[-16,-8.881784197001252e-16,4],"rotation":[0,0.7,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-16,"z":4,"rotY":0.7,"eyebrow":"They are still here","title":"Birds are dinosaurs","body":"Not \"descended from\" — they ARE dinosaurs, the one branch that survived. Every sparrow is a closer relative of T. rex than T. rex was of Triceratops."}},{"id":"856f453d-42c7-4fca-a292-98655bacbb0f","createdAt":1786986001877,"transform":{"position":[-39,-0.037952094134006575,34],"rotation":[0,0.8,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"x":-39,"z":34,"rotY":0.8,"eyebrow":"What happens next","title":"The worst day on Earth","body":"An asteroid about six miles across strikes near what is now Mexico. Within hours the sky is on fire; within years the cold and dark end three quarters of all species. Everything you can see from here goes — except the birds."}},{"id":"0d0ae379-5e09-4495-843e-d79d45937cee","createdAt":1786986001878,"transform":{"position":[-13,5.991681875921475,-45],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"a290b7ab-e70b-4f85-891e-538af9113f23","createdAt":1786986001879,"transform":{"position":[10,8.000000000000009,-40],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"17ec3293-714e-4564-8723-88b3eaab38ce","createdAt":1786986001880,"transform":{"position":[-2,7.883533919513991,-52],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"b95fc87f-4b04-4957-a0f9-9f78226c7739","createdAt":1786986001882,"transform":{"position":[-30,7.000000000000002,-10],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f5f5f5"}]}